   "search_text": "2. Alison has compiled a list of 20 hockey teams, ordered by how good\n   she thinks they are, but refuses to share it. Benjamin may mention\n   three teams to her, and she will then choose either to tell him which\n   she thinks is the weakest team of the three, or which she thinks is the\n   strongest team of the three. Benjamin may do this as many times as\n   he likes. Determine the largest N such that Benjamin can guarantee\n   to be able to ﬁnd a sequence T1, T2, . . . , TN of teams with the property\n   that he knows that Alison thinks that Ti is better than Ti+1 for each\n   1 ≤i < N.\n",
